\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\f0\fs40\lang9 What is Responsible AI (RAI)? \b0\fs22\par
 Responsible AI  refers to the  ethical development, deployment, and governance  of Artificial Intelligence systems to ensure they are  fair, transparent, accountable, and safe .\par
The goal is to build  trustworthy AI  \f1\emdash  systems that people can rely on because they behave as expected and do not cause harm.\par
\par
\b\f0\fs32 Core Principles of Responsible AI \b0\fs22\par
While different organizations (like Microsoft, Google, and OECD) have their own versions, the core Responsible AI principles generally include:\par
1.  \b Fairness  \b0\f1\endash  Ensuring AI does not discriminate against individuals or groups.\par
2. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Transparency and Explainability\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Making AI\rquote s decisions understandable to humans.\par
3. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Accountability\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Ensuring humans are responsible for AI decisions.\par
4. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Privacy and Security\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Protecting user data and ensuring secure systems.\par
5. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Reliability and Safety\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Ensuring AI performs as intended under various conditions.\par
6. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Inclusiveness\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Designing AI systems that benefit everyone.\par
\par
\b\f0\fs40 Bias in AI \fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs32 What is Bias?\fs22\par
\b0 Bias  in AI occurs when a model\f1\rquote\f0 s predictions or behaviors are  systematically unfair  toward certain groups or individuals.\par
It often arises from  imbalanced, incomplete, or skewed training data  or  biased design decisions .\fs32\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b\f1   \f0 Types of Bias\par
\b0\fs22\par
1.  \b Data Bias  \b0\f1\endash  When the training data does not represent the real-world diversity.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example\b0 :\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  A facial recognition system trained mostly on lighter skin tones performs poorly on darker skin tones.\par
\par
2. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Algorithmic Bias\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  When the algorithm amplifies or introduces new unfairness.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example\b0 :\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  An AI recruiting tool that favors male candidates because historical hiring data was biased.\par
\par
3. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Human Bias\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  When developer or annotator assumptions influence the model.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example:\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0 Labeling datasets with subjective human judgments (like \ldblquote attractive\rdblquote  or \ldblquote aggressive\rdblquote ) can embed cultural bias.\par
\par
4. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Societal Bias\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Reflects pre-existing inequalities in society.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example\b0 :\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  Predictive policing systems that disproportionately target minority neighborhoods.\par
\par
\lang16393   \b\f0\fs32\lang9 How to Mitigate Bias\b0\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1   1. Use  diverse and representative datasets .\par
  2. Apply  bias detection tools  (like Fairlearn, IBM AI Fairness 360).\par
  3. Perform  regular audits  and fairness testing.\par
  4. Involve  multidisciplinary teams  (ethicists, domain experts).\par
  5. Ensure  transparency in data collection  and decision logic.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b\fs40 Hallucination in AI \b0\fs22\par
\b\fs32 What is Hallucination?\b0\fs22\par
In the context of AI \f1\emdash  especially \f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 large language models (LLMs)\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \emdash  \lang16393  \lang9 hallucination\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  refers to when the AI \f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 generates information that is plausible-sounding but false, misleading, or unverified\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 .\par
\b\f0 Examples :\b0\par
  An AI writing assistant  inventing fake citations  in research papers.\par
  A chatbot  confidently giving incorrect answers  to factual questions.\par
  A summarization model  adding nonexistent details  to a document.\par
\par
\f1  \b\f0\fs32 Causes of Hallucination\b0\fs22\par
1.  \b Training data noise  \b0\f1\endash  Models learn from inaccurate or incomplete data.\par
2. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Next-token prediction nature\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  LLMs predict the \f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 most likely next word\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 , not necessarily the \f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 truth\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9 .\par
3. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Prompt ambiguity\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Vague or misleading user prompts cause confusion.\par
4. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Lack of grounding\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  The model is not connected to a real-time or verified data source (like a database or API).\par
\par
\b\f0\fs32 Mitigation Strategies\b0\fs22\par
 1. Use  RAG to ground responses in real documents or databases.\par
 2.  Implement  fact-checking mechanisms  before delivering responses.\par
 3.  Encourage  transparency  (e.g., \f1\ldblquote\f0 According to my data\f1\'85\rdblquote\f0 ).\par
 4.  Fine-tune models on  verified and domain-specific data .\par
  5. Allow users to  report or flag hallucinated content .\par
\par
\b\fs40 Explainability in AI \b0\fs22\par
\b\fs32 What is Explainability?\b0\fs22\par
 Explainability  (or  Interpretability ) is the degree to which  humans can understand why and how  an AI system made a particular decision or prediction.\par
\par
\b\fs32 Why It Matters\b0\fs22\par
   1. \b Trust  \b0\f1\endash  Users are more likely to trust AI when they understand its reasoning.\par
\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \f0\lang16393  2. \b\f1\lang9 Accountability\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Helps determine responsibility for outcomes.\par
\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \f0\lang16393  3. \b\f1\lang9 Compliance\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Required by laws (like GDPR\rquote s \ldblquote Right to Explanation\rdblquote ).\par
\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \f0\lang16393 4. \b\f1\lang9 Debugging and improvement\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Developers can detect model weaknesses.\par
\par
\b\f0\fs32 Levels of Explainability\b0\fs22\par
1.  \b Global Explainability  \b0\f1\endash  Understanding the overall model behavior.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example\b0 :\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  Feature importance plots showing which inputs influence outcomes.\par
2. \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Local Explainability\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \b0\endash  Explaining individual predictions.\par
   \f0\lang16393  \b\f1\lang9 Example\b0 :\f0\lang16393  \f1\lang9  \ldblquote The model rejected your loan because of low income and high debt ratio.\rdblquote\par
\par
\b\f0\fs32 Techniques for Explainability\par
\b0\fs22    \b Model-agnostic tools: \b0\par
     LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations) \par
\f1     \f2\u8594?\f1  \f0 Creates local approximations of complex models to show how features affect decisions.\par
     SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) \par
\f1     \f2\u8594?\f1  \f0 Assigns each feature a contribution value for a given prediction.\par
\par
   \b Model-specific approaches: \b0\par
     Decision Trees  \f2\u8594?\f1  \f0 inherently explainable.\par
     Attention visualization  in neural networks.\par
     Saliency maps  for image models (show which pixels influenced the prediction).\par
\par
\b Trade-off\b0\par
There\f1\rquote\f0 s often a  trade-off between model accuracy and interpretability :\par
  1. Simpler models (like linear regression) are more explainable but less powerful.\par
  2. Complex models (like deep neural networks) are more accurate but harder to interpret.\f1\par
